WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.780
Hidden away in the mountains of&nbsp;
northern New Mexico lay one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.720
the most secretive military projects in history.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.320
The Manhattan Project was&nbsp;
the codename for the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.740
government’s program to develop&nbsp;
the world’s first atomic bomb.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.560
It was so secretive that President Roosevelt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.380
wasn’t fully informed of&nbsp;
all the technical details.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.940
Vice-President Truman didn’t know it&nbsp;
existed until after he became president.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.620
Many members of Congress didn’t&nbsp;
know it existed until after WWII.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.680
The bomb was assembled in Los Alamos, New Mexico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.480
which housed the main research laboratory&nbsp;
for developing and testing nuclear weapons.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.680
Scientists worked to harness the&nbsp;
energy of the atom to build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.680
most destructive weapon humankind had ever seen.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.480
And they had to move quickly.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.600
In 1938, scientists in a lab in Berlin&nbsp;
had managed to split the uranium atom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.340
The energy released from the splitting -&nbsp;
or fission - is enough to power a bomb.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.120
Einstein was worried that the&nbsp;
Nazis might be developing nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.700
weapons so he sent a letter to Roosevelt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:07.500
which convinced the President to initiate&nbsp;
the American effort to build an atomic bomb.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.180
In a bizarre twist, Einstein was actually&nbsp;
forbidden from working on the Manhattan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:16.920
Project because he was a left-leaning political&nbsp;
activist and deemed a potential security risk.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.980
General Leslie Groves appointed one of the world’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.320
top theoretical physicists to lead the&nbsp;
Manhattan Project, which began in 1943.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:29.640
J. Robert Oppenheimer was a well-known&nbsp;
instructor at both the University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.480
of California at Berkeley and the&nbsp;
California Institute of Technology.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.600
He handpicked most of the Los Alamos staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:42.720
personally traveling to the best universities&nbsp;
across America to recruit top scientists.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.580
Oppenheimer chose New Mexico for the site’s&nbsp;
location because of its natural beauty.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:53.220
Los Alamos, in particular, was ideal for a&nbsp;
top-secret project because it’s situated on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.860
an isolated plateau. And the sparsely populated&nbsp;
location was safer in case of a nuclear accident.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.700
The U.S. army purchased over 40,000 acres of land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.380
surrounding a private all-boys prep school&nbsp;
and immediately began to build a community.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:13.260
At its peak, about 10,000 scientists,&nbsp;
engineers, technicians, construction workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:16.740
military personnel, and their&nbsp;
families lived and worked there.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.940
They enjoyed hikes throughout the area&nbsp;
and attended parties on the weekends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.820
at scientists’ residences in the gated facility.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.500
A hodge podge of laboratory&nbsp;
buildings sprouted up at Los Alamos.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.540
Most of the equipment was transported by the&nbsp;
scientists themselves to maintain secrecy.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.580
On paper, it was only referred to as “Project&nbsp;
Y”, operated by the University of California.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.460
And no one who went to live and work there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:53.040
allowed to tell their family or&nbsp;
friends where they were going.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:59.520
All incoming mail was addressed to the&nbsp;
same place: P.O. Box 1663, Santa Fe.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.240
Babies born there actually had&nbsp;
that on their birth certificate.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.260
Los Alamos wasn’t the only&nbsp;
location for the Manhattan Project.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.940
In Oak Ridge, Tennessee, scientists&nbsp;
enriched uranium to fuel a bomb.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.000
In Washington state, reactors were built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.980
to produce plutonium…an entirely new&nbsp;
substance that could also fuel a bomb.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.420
If you worked at one site, you likely&nbsp;
didn’t have any idea what was being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.500
done elsewhere or even what the&nbsp;
overall goal of the project was.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.800
I didn’t know what I was&nbsp;
doing, I was just doing it.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.700
Ruth Huddleston was 18 years old when&nbsp;
she joined the Manhattan Project.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:41.280
They told us when I was in training&nbsp;
that we were not supposed to ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.440
let anything leave that room. We&nbsp;
were not supposed to tell anybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.480
what we were doing…not even to the&nbsp;
people that we were working with.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.880
Ruth was one of 10,000 young women who were&nbsp;
responsible for ensuring that a large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.180
complex machine called a calutron separated&nbsp;
isotopes of uranium, which fuelled the bomb.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:09.780
Calutrons separated lighter uranium 235 from&nbsp;
the heavier and more common uranium 238.&nbsp;

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:16.200
U-235 is highly unstable, which makes it&nbsp;
splittable or fissionable. When it’s slammed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:22.980
a neutron, it becomes U236. It’s so unstable that&nbsp;
it splits into two atoms. In the process, more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:29.640
neutrons are released. They slam into more U-235s,&nbsp;
causing a chain reaction and a huge explosion.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:33.660
Ruth’s job was to monitor the&nbsp;
meters on the calutron machine.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.120
That’s her seated third back on the left.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:42.480
They had a certain range that&nbsp;
they wanted that meter to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.580
in. They told us if it went too far&nbsp;
to the left or too far to the right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.500
it was time for us to straighten it&nbsp;
out and get it back in that range

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.140
Her job would have normally gone to a scientist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.520
but there weren’t enough of them, and&nbsp;
young men were fighting overseas. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.960
the government recruited farm girls, most of&nbsp;
whom were hired straight out of high school.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.740
And they proved to be better&nbsp;
at the job than scientists!

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.980
Scientists were too concerned with&nbsp;
figuring out what had gone wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:15.480
whereas these young women would simply alert&nbsp;
their supervisors when there was an issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.860
They also didn’t overthink any adjustments&nbsp;
that were needed, as a scientist might.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.840
If they thought too much and started&nbsp;
asking questions, there were consequences.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.460
If somebody was being nosey and&nbsp;
wanting to ask you everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.680
and wanted to talk too much about&nbsp;
it, for some reason they disappeared.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.260
They lost their jobs.
On July 16, 1945,

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:46.500
Los Alamos scientists detonated the world’s&nbsp;
first nuclear weapon over the New Mexico desert.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.300
The implosion-type device with a&nbsp;
plutonium core looked like a steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.680
globe and was nicknamed the “Gadget”&nbsp;
- which again speaks to its secrecy.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.040
It was similar in design to&nbsp;
the fat man bomb dropped on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.680
the Japanese city of Nagasaki three weeks later.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:07.680
Another atomic bomb had just devastated&nbsp;
the Japanese city of Hiroshima.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.380
The bombs killed hundreds of thousands of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:15.360
from exposure to the blasts and also from&nbsp;
the long-term side effects of radiation.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.260
When they first told us, when I first heard it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:27.780
it really bothered me, it upset me because I&nbsp;
realized that I had helped kill a lot of people.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:33.720
The horror of two atomic bombs convinced&nbsp;
Japanese hardliners to surrender.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.200
When the Manhattan Project was&nbsp;
finally revealed to the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.180
many were shocked to learn that such&nbsp;
a large operation existed in secret.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:48.480
At its peak, the Manhattan Project employed&nbsp;
130,000 people and, by the end of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.640
it had cost $2.2 billion - the&nbsp;
equivalent of $36 billion today.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:57.780
The Manhattan Project ultimately&nbsp;
led to the Cold War arms race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:01.080
which brought with it the&nbsp;
possibility of armageddon.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.200
And that possibility haunts the world today.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.380
The best and brightest scientists worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.260
on the Manhattan Project and&nbsp;
changed the course of history.

